% ── Abstract ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\begin{center}
\textbf{Abstract}
\end{center}

A thermal optimum migrates on decadal timescales; the terrain configuration that gives a terroir-bound crop its identity is fixed on geological ones. Climate-only suitability projections implicitly offer adaptation by relocation, but where a product's value derives from particular ground, the optimum can move and the crop cannot follow. We formalise this as the intersection of a moving thermal set with a stationary terrain set---a terrain-thermal convergence framework combining a Gaussian thermal niche with a district-scale, identity-anchored topographic feasibility screen---and apply it to Hawaiian specialty coffee's two producing districts, Kona and Ka\okina u, on a 500\,m island-wide grid ($n = 471$ labelled farm cells). The two districts' thermal optima are statistically indistinguishable (difference $0.18$\,\textdegree C, 95\% CI $-0.37$ to $+0.02$\,\textdegree C)---a climate-only projection cannot separate them---yet their terrain footprints are small and fixed (5.9\% of the Big Island matches Kona's agro-ecological profile and 8.9\% matches Ka\okina u's under the primary screen; 6.1\% and 3.8\% under a decorrelated distance metric): the two districts diverge through terrain, not thermal sensitivity. Evaluated across the full farmable island surface, warming appears to increase thermal suitability for roughly 70\% of farmable land under either district's envelope---a figure we show is equivalent, to within one percent, to the statement that 70\% of farmable land lies above $\approx$600\,m, and therefore a description of the island's hypsometry rather than an independent climatic result. Restricted to each district's own terrain the sign reverses: 72\% of Kona's and 77\% of Ka\okina u's labelled farm cells are on a declining thermal trajectory by 2035, rising to 79\% and 86\% by 2045. The mechanism is visible in the horizon-to-horizon change: between 2035 and 2045 the island-wide thermally suitable area is static to within one percent and the terrain footprint is unchanged by construction, yet their intersection contracts by 15.5\% and 12.4\%---neither set shrinks, but the moving one slides off the fixed one. Terrain-constrained suitable area is several-fold smaller than the thermal-only estimate, but comparison against a size-matched null shows that this multiplier reflects the size and placement of the terrain footprint rather than any property of the districts---random patches of equal area yield larger factors 97--100\% of the time---so we report it as a sign, not a magnitude. For a terroir-bound crop the upslope migration that climate-only projections imply is therefore largely unavailable, and the operative question under warming is not how much land becomes climatically suitable but how long the suitable band and the identity-bearing terrain remain in register.

