\subsection{The Thermal Optimum Migrates Upslope (and What That Statistic Is Not)}
\label{sec:results:thermal}

Figure~\ref{fig:thermal} shows thermal suitability change across the full farmable island surface, evaluated by regional envelope without applying the terrain screen. Under the Kona envelope, 71.2\% of farmable cells gain thermal suitability by 2035 (69.8\% by 2045); under the Ka\okina u envelope, 69.8\% gain by 2035 (68.4\% by 2045). Because the two envelopes' optima are statistically indistinguishable (\S\ref{sec:studyarea}), the climate-only trajectories are nearly indistinguishable at the island-wide scale shown here---the districts diverge only once each is restricted to its own terrain footprint (\S\ref{sec:results:overestimation}).

These island-wide fractions should not be over-read, and we state their content plainly. Since $\Delta S_r > 0 \iff T < \mu_r - \Delta T_h/2$ (\S\ref{sec:methods}) and the temperature field is $98\%$ explained by elevation (\S\ref{sec:methods}), each fraction is equivalent to a single elevation contour: gain under the Kona envelope at 2035 means $T < 20.50$\,\textdegree C, i.e.\ $z \gtrsim 558$\,m, and the two classifications agree on 98.9\% of farmable cells (Ka\okina u 2035: $z \gtrsim 592$\,m, 99.1\% agreement; both districts at 2045: $z \gtrsim 592$\,m and $627$\,m, $\geq$99.1\%). ``Roughly 70\% of farmable land gains'' is therefore, to within about one percent, the statement ``roughly 70\% of farmable Big Island land lies above $\approx$560--630\,m''---a fact about the hypsometry of a shield volcano, not an independent climatic finding. It is reported here because it is the quantity a climate-only assessment would produce, and the contrast with the terrain-restricted result (\S\ref{sec:results:convergence}) is the paper's subject; it is not itself evidence of anything.

