\subsection{What Remains: The Feasible Set Under Warming}
\label{sec:results:convergence}

Applying the topographic screen collapses the apparent thermal opportunity into a much smaller feasible domain, and reveals a pattern common to both districts rather than one unique to either (Figure~\ref{fig:convergence}).

Within Kona-like terrain, screened cells are predominantly blue at the lower, coastal-facing portion of the belt---where most existing farms sit (Figure~\ref{fig:thermal}a farm markers)---with a red, gaining fringe concentrated at the upper elevation margin. Overall, 69.0\% of Kona-like farmable terrain is on a declining thermal trajectory by 2035 (71.7\% by 2045); the remaining 31.0\%/28.3\% gains, concentrated in that upper fringe.

Within Ka\okina u-like terrain, the same pattern recurs: a lower, red-to-blue transition near the coast where warming has already pushed conditions past the optimum, and gains concentrated toward the upper margin. Overall, 59.3\% of Ka\okina u-like farmable terrain is on a declining thermal trajectory by 2035 (63.9\% by 2045); the remaining 40.7\%/36.1\% gains. Ka\okina u's declining majority is smaller than Kona's, but this is the one result in the paper that does not survive a change of distance metric: under the Mahalanobis screen the declining fractions fall to 62.4\% (Kona) and 50.2\% (Ka\okina u) at 2035, so Ka\okina u's majority becomes a near-even split. We report the difference but do not build on it.

The central result of this study is what happens to that pattern when it is evaluated on land that is actually farmed rather than land that merely matches the district's terrain profile. Existing farms are not distributed uniformly across their district's footprint. In Kona they sit preferentially in its lower, warmer portion, which is where the optimum departs first. Ka\okina u's footprint (185--895\,m) extends both above and below its farms (250--750\,m), so the effect there is not simply that farms occupy the low end; rather, the farm distribution is narrower than the terrain envelope and sits closer to its warm side. In both districts the net result is the same in sign: farm cells decline faster than the terrain footprint containing them. Evaluated on the 409 labelled Kona farm cells, \textbf{72.1\%} are on a declining thermal trajectory by 2035 (95\% CI 66.0--77.9), rising to \textbf{78.5\%} by 2045 (72.7--84.3); for Ka\okina u's 62 farm cells the figures are \textbf{77.4\%} (57.1--93.8) and \textbf{85.5\%} (64.7--98.9). Intervals are spatial block bootstraps (\S\ref{sec:methods:uncertainty}); Ka\okina u's are wide because its 62 farm cells form only about eight spatially independent blocks. Evaluated per emissions pathway rather than at the reference increment, the 2035 figures span 64.1--78.7\% for Kona and 69.4--85.5\% for Ka\okina u between SSP2-4.5 and SSP5-8.5, rising to 68.7--85.6\% and 75.8--91.9\% at 2045 (Supplementary Table~S2). The direction is identical under both pathways; only the magnitude differs. Converting these to acreage requires care, and the naive conversion is biased. Multiplying a cell fraction by district acreage assumes every labelled cell contains the same coffee area, which it does not. Intersecting the mapped farm polygons with the labelled cells and weighting each cell by the coffee area it actually contains moves the 2035 figures to 68.0\% for Kona (from 72.1\%) and 81.8\% for Ka\okina u (from 77.4\%), and the 2045 figures to 74.7\% and 90.4\%---by roughly four percentage points in each district, in opposite directions, because Kona's larger farm cells sit lower in the belt while Ka\okina u's sit higher. On an area-weighted basis this corresponds to roughly 3{,}265 of Kona's $\approx$4{,}800 farmed acres and 982 of Ka\okina u's $\approx$1{,}200 by 2035. We note additionally that the polygons falling inside labelled cells total $\approx$3{,}795 acres against the $\approx$6{,}000 acres mapped island-wide, because cells were labelled by farm-centroid overlap and partially covered cells are not counted; district acreages quoted here are therefore approximate scalings rather than direct measurements. These are larger than the terrain-level fractions above precisely because the terrain footprint includes upper-elevation ground that farms do not currently occupy.

That upper ground is the only direction the optimum is moving, and it is where the argument of the paper bites. The gaining fringe exists, but it lies at the upper margin of a footprint that does not extend further upslope: beyond it, terrain stops resembling Kona or Ka\okina u---the slope steepens onto the volcano's upper constructional flanks, aspect and relief depart from the observed farm envelope, and substrate ages fall away. Warming therefore does not open new coffee country; it relocates the favourable band within a fixed one, and the band is running out of footprint to move into.

\subsection{The Contraction Mechanism: A Sliding Band on a Fixed Footprint}
\label{sec:results:contraction}

The preceding sections establish two facts that do not interact until they are put together: the terrain footprint is fixed (\S\ref{sec:results:terrain}) and the thermally favourable band moves (\S\ref{sec:results:thermal}). This section states the consequence, which is the paper's central claim and the one result that survives every robustness check reported below.

Because temperature is effectively a function of elevation within the belt (\S\ref{sec:methods}), this mechanism admits an explicit one-dimensional statement, and we give it rather than leave it to be inferred. At $\tau = 0.5$ the level set is $|T + \Delta T - \mu_r| < \sqrt{2\ln 2}\,\sigma_r$, a band of fixed width in temperature and therefore---at the within-belt lapse of $-5.72$\,\textdegree C\,km$^{-1}$---of fixed width in elevation: $\pm282$\,m for Kona, $\pm214$\,m for Ka\okina u. Warming translates that band upslope by $\Delta T / 5.72$\,km without changing its width, while the terrain footprint stays put. The feasible set is then simply the overlap between a sliding interval and a fixed one. For Kona (footprint $150$--$950$\,m) the band runs $388$--$952$\,m at $+1.00$\,\textdegree C and $449$--$1{,}014$\,m at $+1.35$\,\textdegree C; clipped to the footprint, the overlap falls from $562$ to $501$\,m, a predicted contraction of $10.9\%$. For Ka\okina u the same construction predicts $15.0\%$.

Against those one-dimensional predictions the full two-dimensional analysis returns $-15.5\%$ for Kona and $-12.4\%$ for Ka\okina u. Real terrain therefore contributes $-4.7$ percentage points for Kona and $+2.6$ for Ka\okina u---in opposite directions, which is why the two-dimensional computation is not redundant with the interval model even though the interval model captures most of the effect. The difference arises from hypsometry (on a shield volcano, area is not distributed uniformly with elevation, so equal elevation intervals do not contain equal area) and from the footprints' irregular shape. We regard the honest description of this result as a one-dimensional geometric argument validated on real terrain, rather than as a spatial finding that happens to admit a simple explanation.

The clearest expression of this is what happens between the two horizons. Island-wide, the thermally suitable area is essentially unchanged from 2035 to 2045---it falls 0.6\% under the Kona envelope and \textit{rises} 0.5\% under Ka\okina u's---and the terrain set, by construction, does not change at all. Yet the feasible set, their intersection, contracts by 15.5\% (Kona, 1{,}371 to 1{,}158 cells) and 12.4\% (Ka\okina u, 1{,}839 to 1{,}611). Neither input shrinks; the intersection does, because the moving set is sliding off the stationary one. The same holds under each emissions pathway taken separately, and more starkly under the lower one: under SSP2-4.5 the island-wide thermal set \textit{grows} between horizons ($+0.6\%$ Kona, $+1.1\%$ Ka\okina u) while the intersection contracts ($-7.9\%$ and $-5.6\%$); under SSP5-8.5 the thermal set is flat ($-1.0\%$, $-0.4\%$) and the intersection contracts far harder ($-17.4\%$ and $-20.0\%$; Supplementary Table~S2). More thermally suitable land island-wide, less usable land inside the districts, is the clearest statement of the effect this paper describes. No amount of additional warming-driven thermal opportunity elsewhere on the island can compensate, because that opportunity is not on terrain that is Kona or Ka\okina u.

