\subsection{Caveat: Why the Overestimation Multiplier Is Not the Result}
\label{sec:results:overestimation}

A natural way to summarise the preceding sections would be a single ratio: thermally suitable area divided by terrain-constrained suitable area, reported as the factor by which a climate-only assessment overstates accessible adaptation space. Earlier versions of this analysis did exactly that. We report the ratio below for completeness and because it is the quantity a reader will otherwise compute, but we argue in this section that it should not be treated as the study's result, and we present the evidence that persuaded us of this. The finding of the paper is the structural one in \S\ref{sec:results:convergence}---a moving thermal set sliding off a stationary terrain set---not a multiplier. We define:
\[
    \text{Overestimation factor} =
    \frac{|\mathcal{C}_r(\Delta T)|}{|\mathcal{F}_r(\Delta T)|}, \qquad
    \mathcal{C}_r(\Delta T) = \{g \in \mathcal{G} : S_r(T_g, \Delta T) > \tau\}, \quad \tau = 0.5
\]
using the level-set thermal suitability set $\mathcal{C}_r(\Delta T)$ (\S\ref{sec:methods}), not the direction-of-change statistic $\Delta S_r > 0$ reported in \S\ref{sec:results:convergence}'s district-specific trajectories below.

This ratio decomposes into two multiplicative terms: the ratio of thermal-only to terrain-constrained \textit{suitability rates} (the fraction of all farmable island cells exceeding $\tau$, divided by the fraction within the district's own terrain footprint exceeding $\tau$), times the inverse of the terrain fraction itself---an input taken directly from the farm-polygon data, not a finding of the thermal analysis. At the 95th-percentile baseline for 2035: for Kona, $6.9 = 0.468 \times 14.7$; for Ka\okina u, $3.9 = 0.342 \times 11.3$, where 14.7 and 11.3 are each district's $|\mathcal{G}|/|\mathcal{T}_r|$ (the inverse of its terrain fraction among farmable cells). Unlike a naive terrain-fraction bound, the terrain-thermal interaction term here (0.468, 0.342) is \textit{less than one}: each district's own terrain is already substantially more likely to be absolutely suitable than farmable land generally (55.8\% of Kona-like terrain exceeds $\tau$ versus 26.1\% of all farmable land; 57.9\% of Ka\okina u-like terrain versus 19.8\% island-wide)---because farms were sited, in the first place, on terrain that already sits closer to the district's own thermal optimum. The realized factor is therefore \textit{smaller} than the terrain-fraction-alone bound would predict, not larger, and the headline multiplier should be read as evidence for the terrain-thermal convergence framework's central claim---that suitability is a restricted intersection, not a continuous surface---rather than as a free-standing estimate of misdirected adaptation investment.

\textit{Ka\okina u.} Under the Ka\okina u envelope at $\sim$2035, 7{,}136 of the 36{,}055 farmable island cells (19.8\%) exceed the $\tau=0.5$ suitability threshold under future warming. Restricted to Ka\okina u-like terrain, 1{,}839 cells (5.1\% of farmable terrain) exceed it: overestimation factor = \textbf{3.9} at the 95th-percentile baseline (95\% CI 3.2--8.6; 3.5$\times$ under SSP2-4.5 and 4.6$\times$ under SSP5-8.5, Supplementary Table~S2). Across $\tau=0.1$--$0.8$, the factor ranges 3.5$\times$--4.7$\times$; across the terrain-threshold sensitivity range (90th--97.5th percentile) it ranges 3.2$\times$--6.7$\times$ under Euclidean distance and 8.9$\times$--27.7$\times$ under Mahalanobis distance (Supplementary Table~S1)---an order-of-magnitude overestimation does not hold at every threshold tested. By the 2045 horizon, thermal-only suitable area is 7{,}173 cells (19.9\%) and terrain-constrained suitable area is 1{,}611 cells (4.5\%), overestimation factor \textbf{4.5}. Restricted to Ka\okina u-like terrain specifically (3{,}177 farmable cells), 59.3\% is on a \textit{declining} thermal trajectory by 2035 (63.9\% by 2045). Evaluated instead on the district's 62 \textit{labelled farm cells}---the quantity relevant to existing growers---the decline fraction is substantially higher: 77.4\% by 2035 and 85.5\% by 2045, or roughly 929 to 1{,}026 of the district's $\approx$1{,}200 farmed acres. Farm cells decline faster than the terrain footprint as a whole because farms sit preferentially in the lower, warmer portion of the belt (\S\ref{sec:results:convergence}); applying a terrain-level fraction to farmed acreage would understate the exposure of existing production. This direction-of-change statistic is unaffected by the $\tau$ threshold (\S\ref{sec:methods}).

\textit{Kona.} 9{,}415 of the 36{,}055 farmable island cells (26.1\%) exceed $\tau=0.5$ under the Kona envelope at $\sim$2035---a higher rate than Ka\okina u's, since Kona's envelope is centred slightly warmer and therefore closer to typical low-elevation Big Island temperatures. Restricted to Kona-like terrain, 1{,}371 cells exceed it---3.8\% of all farmable island cells, or 55.8\% of the Kona-like footprint itself: overestimation factor = \textbf{6.9} at the 95th-percentile baseline (95\% CI 6.3--9.1; 6.1$\times$ under SSP2-4.5 and 8.3$\times$ under SSP5-8.5, Supplementary Table~S2), rising to \textbf{8.1} by 2045 (9{,}363 cells thermal-only, 1{,}158 terrain-constrained). Across $\tau=0.1$--$0.8$, the factor ranges 6.9$\times$--7.4$\times$, and across the terrain-threshold range (90th--97.5th percentile) 6.3$\times$--8.8$\times$. Restricted to Kona-like terrain specifically (2{,}456 farmable cells), 69.0\% is on a declining thermal trajectory by 2035 (71.7\% by 2045); on the district's 409 labelled farm cells the figure is again higher---72.1\% by 2035 and 78.5\% by 2045, roughly 3{,}462 to 3{,}767 of the district's $\approx$4{,}800 farmed acres. The gaining minority concentrates at the upper elevation margin of the belt. Because the topographic screen's own feature set includes mean elevation (\S\ref{sec:methods:topo}), a cell that moves far enough upslope to gain thermal suitability necessarily also moves away from the Kona centroid in elevation space; whether that upper-fringe gain reflects a genuine agro-ecological refuge or is partly an artefact of including elevation directly in the screen is a question this analysis does not resolve (\S\ref{sec:discussion:limits}).

The overestimation is a geometric consequence of evaluating suitability over a continuous surface when the realizable niche occupies a restricted terrain subset, but its magnitude is not a stable property of either district, and we recommend against reading it as one. It is not invariant to threshold or distance-metric choice, spanning 3.2$\times$--27.7$\times$ for Ka\okina u across defensible alternatives (Supplementary Table~S1); it is mildly sensitive to the $1.5\times$ envelope-width inflation (\S\ref{sec:methods}), moving from 6.9$\times$ to 7.1$\times$ (Kona) and 3.6$\times$ to 4.2$\times$ (Ka\okina u) as the inflation runs from $1.0\times$ to $2.0\times$; and, most importantly, it is not distinctive of the districts at all.

To test the last point we constructed null distributions of the factor. An initial null drew 200 random compact patches of farmable land matched to each district on area alone; against it the observed factors sat at the extreme low end (Kona 6.9$\times$ against a null median of 12.5$\times$, with 95\% of patches yielding a larger factor; Ka\okina u 3.9$\times$ against 9.6$\times$, 100\%). That construction is confounded. Kona and Ka\okina u are elongated, elevation-banded belts, whereas a compact patch of equal area on a shield volcano necessarily spans a wider elevation range; since temperature is effectively a function of elevation at this scale (\S\ref{sec:methods}), a wider elevation range mechanically lowers the fraction of the patch exceeding $\tau$ and inflates the factor. The apparent distinctiveness of the districts was therefore substantially an artefact of patch \textit{shape}.

We replaced it with a null matched on the quantity that actually drives the statistic: 2{,}000 draws of farmable cells stratified to reproduce each district's own elevation histogram, without any contiguity constraint. Under this null the observed factors are essentially indistinguishable from random terrain---Kona 6.87$\times$ against a null median of 7.01$\times$ (5th--95th percentile 6.94--7.07), Ka\okina u 3.88$\times$ against 3.89$\times$ (3.87--3.92). The districts sit marginally below their respective null medians, by about 2\% for Kona and under 1\% for Ka\okina u. We therefore do not claim that these districts are unusually well sited; the earlier reading, based on the unmatched null, does not survive.

The width of that null interval is itself the most informative result here. Once the elevation distribution of a candidate footprint is fixed, the overestimation factor is determined to within roughly one percent, irrespective of where on the island the cells lie or what shape they form. The factor is not a measurement of an agro-ecological property of Kona or Ka\okina u. It is a function of the elevation histogram of whatever terrain is screened, and the districts' histograms are what set their values.

What survives is the qualitative structure, not the multiplier: terrain-constrained suitable area is several-fold smaller than the thermal-only estimate under every setting tested, for both districts, which is the sign of the effect and the reason a feasibility screen is necessary at all.

% ── 4. Discussion ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Discussion}

A thermal optimum that migrates upslope is only an adaptation opportunity if there is upslope land the crop can actually occupy. For a terroir-bound crop there generally is not, because the terrain configuration that defines the product is fixed on a timescale orders of magnitude longer than the one on which the optimum moves. That mismatch is the paper's argument, and Hawaiian coffee makes it unusually legible: between 2035 and 2045 the island-wide thermally suitable area is flat to within one percent and the terrain footprint is unchanged by construction, yet the intersection of the two---the land that is simultaneously warm enough and actually Kona or Ka\okina u---contracts by 16--21\% (\S\ref{sec:results:convergence}). Nothing shrank except the alignment between a set that moves and a set that cannot.

The consequence for existing production is direct: 72\% of Kona's and 77\% of Ka\okina u's labelled farm cells are on a declining thermal trajectory by 2035, rising to 79\% and 86\% by 2045. These fractions exceed the corresponding terrain-level fractions because farms occupy the lower, warmer part of each belt, and they are the numbers relevant to growers and lenders rather than to a land-area accounting. The implication extends beyond the case study: wherever cultivable terrain is spatially structured---confined to specific soil types, aspect regimes, parent materials, or slope configurations---climate-only projections will overstate accessible adaptation space \citep{Hijmans2005, Elith2009, Phillips2006, Bunn2015, OvalleRivera2015}. Previous work has noted that climate suitability projections can substantially overestimate available agricultural land where non-climatic constraints such as land use, accessibility, and terrain are not explicitly represented \citep{Bunn2015, OvalleRivera2015}. The degree of overestimation scales with the geographic specificity of the terrain requirement; in orographic specialty crop systems, that specificity is extreme.

The two districts face the same version of this problem, not structurally different ones. \textbf{Kona and Ka\okina u are both spatially constrained.} Their thermal optima are statistically indistinguishable (\S\ref{sec:studyarea}), so a shared warming signal produces nearly identical island-wide thermal trajectories for both districts (\S\ref{sec:results:thermal}). What differs is not thermal sensitivity but terrain geometry: each district occupies a compact, elevation-banded footprint with no room to expand, and in both cases warming shifts the thermally-favourable zone toward the upper margin of that fixed footprint, leaving the lower, currently-farmed majority of each belt on a declining trajectory. The risk is structural for both, not exceptional to either: not merely that farms are warming, but that the terrain type those farms occupy limits how much of each belt can track the thermal optimum upslope. The thermal window shifts for both districts; the geomorphological door widens for neither.

For practitioners in both districts, the convergence map translates this shared structural constraint into concrete planning guidance. For Kona and Ka\okina u producers alike, the priority is thermal resilience within the existing topographic footprint: shade management to reduce canopy temperatures, variety trials with higher thermal tolerance, and targeted monitoring at the upper-elevation fringe where the terrain-thermal intersection remains positive through 2035--2045. The convergence map (Figure~\ref{fig:convergence}) identifies those fringe cells explicitly for both districts---priority sites for agronomic intervention, not confirmed relocation or expansion targets (\S\ref{sec:discussion:limits}). Large-scale geographic relocation is constrained by the geometry of each belt; the actionable adaptation space is smaller than unconstrained thermal analysis suggests, for Kona and Ka\okina u alike. Any non-farmed, topographically matched, thermally-gaining cells identified for either district are candidates for expansion feasibility studies only after terrain, soil, and access screening---thermal trajectory second, not first.

The economic stakes follow directly from the terrain geometry, and apply symmetrically precisely because the two districts' thermal niches barely differ (\S\ref{sec:studyarea})---the risk is shared, not district-specific. Roughly 69\% of Kona-appropriate terrain and 59\% of Ka\okina u-appropriate terrain is on a declining thermal trajectory by 2035. Evaluated on the labelled farm cells themselves the figures are higher and closer together---72\% and 77\%---representing roughly 3,462 of Kona's $\approx$4,800 farmed acres and 929 of Ka\okina u's $\approx$1,200. Because ``100\% Kona'' certification commands premium wholesale prices and the premium is inseparable from geographic identity \citep{Lee2023}, thermal resilience in place---shade management, heat-tolerant variety trials---is not a contingency plan but the only viable plan, for both districts. The deeper economic risk is misallocation of adaptation capital driven by a false signal: a planner relying on the unscreened, thermal-only direction-of-change view observes roughly 70\% of farmable island land apparently gaining suitability under either district's envelope, and may rationally treat large-scale relocation or expansion as a near-term option. Restricted to each district's own terrain, the majority is instead on a declining trajectory, and on the farm cells themselves roughly three-quarters (\S\ref{sec:results:overestimation}), and a genuine absolute-suitability accounting (\S\ref{sec:methods}) puts terrain-constrained accessible land at 4- to 8-fold less than the thermal-only estimate. Most of that gap is arithmetic---each district's terrain footprint is a small, fixed fraction of the island to begin with (\S\ref{sec:results:overestimation})---but the practical implication does not depend on how the gap decomposes: wherever specialty crop premiums are anchored to geographic identity, the price signal will misprice supply risk unless land-use and investment decisions are read against actual terrain, not thermal projections alone.

Two biotic constraints lie outside this framework entirely, and one of them acts specifically on the terrain the analysis identifies as gaining. Coffee berry borer (\textit{Hypothenemus hampei}) reached Kona in September 2010 and Ka\okina u in May 2011, and in the two seasons following its arrival was associated with an estimated \$25.7 million in lost sales and a statewide yield decline of roughly 20\% \citep{Lee2023}. Its development rate on Hawai\okina i Island is strongly elevation-dependent: across 204--778\,m, \citet{Hamilton2019} fit generations per month as a linear decline with elevation ($R^2 = 0.89$), corresponding to roughly 4.1--5.0 generations per fruiting season at 200--300\,m against 2.1--3.3 at 600--780\,m. Because that gradient is temperature-mediated---the same study frames development in degree-day terms---warming is thermally equivalent to a downslope shift---at the within-belt lapse of $-5.72$\,\textdegree C\,km$^{-1}$ (\S\ref{sec:methods}), $1$\,\textdegree C corresponds to $175$\,m---and applying their fitted relationship under the increments used here implies generation-rate increases of approximately 34\% at 600\,m and 51\% at 800\,m by 2035, and 46\% and 69\% by 2045. This calculation is ours rather than theirs, extrapolates their relationship slightly beyond its upper sampling limit, and holds constant everything else that covaries with elevation; we present it as an order-of-magnitude indication, not a projection. Its direction, however, is unambiguous and unfavourable: the proportional increase is largest where the current generation rate is lowest, which is to say at the upper-elevation margin this analysis identifies as the only thermally gaining terrain. The fringe that gains thermal suitability is also the fringe that gains pest pressure fastest, and the two effects are driven by the same warming.

Coffee leaf rust (\textit{Hemileia vastatrix}) is a second, differently structured omission. It was confirmed on Maui on 30 October 2020---the first detection in the United States---and was present on all major Hawaiian islands within nine months \citep{HDOA2021CLR}. It postdates the start of the baseline period used here, so no part of the observed record reflects its presence. More importantly, its infection requirement is free moisture on the leaf rather than a temperature threshold, and this analysis does not project precipitation (below). Coffee leaf rust risk therefore sits outside the model in a way that no thermal screen can capture, and the upper-elevation fringe's higher cloud and moisture exposure is a plausible aggravating factor rather than a mitigating one.

Taken together these constraints do not undermine the paper's structural argument---a fixed terrain footprint still cannot track a moving thermal optimum---but they do bound the interpretation of the gaining fringe. That fringe should be read as \textit{thermally} favourable terrain, not as a refuge in any agronomic sense. Establishing whether it is genuinely available would require joint modelling of pest and pathogen pressure alongside the terrain and thermal screens, which we regard as the single most valuable extension of this work.

Several limitations constrain these estimates. \label{sec:discussion:limits} The most consequential is the one quantified in \S\ref{sec:methods}: the temperature field is 98\% explained by elevation, and the topographic screen uses elevation as one of its features, so the thermal and terrain screens are strongly correlated rather than independent. Everything downstream inherits this. In particular, the finding that the gaining fraction of each district's terrain concentrates at the upper elevation margin cannot be cleanly separated from the fact that moving upslope also moves a cell away from its district's elevation centroid; the analysis can show that the thermally-favourable zone migrates upslope while the terrain envelope does not, but it cannot establish that any particular upper-fringe cell constitutes a genuine agro-ecological refuge. A screen built on non-elevation terrain descriptors, or a thermal field with substantially more non-elevation structure than the residual $0.60$\,\textdegree C available here, would be required to resolve that. The observed climatology also does not resolve canopy-level thermal modification by shade trees, which acts at a finer scale than any gridded climate product. Fibre-optic distributed temperature sensing (DTS) along elevation transects would provide the in-situ calibration data needed to validate the gridded climatology against observed canopy conditions and remains a natural next step. The climatology used here is the Kodama product's full 1990--2021 record (its complete available temporal extent), so it does not share a baseline period with the 2017--2025 window used for the ERA5-Land-derived warming signal $\Delta T$ (\S\ref{sec:data}); reconciling the two periods exactly, rather than combining a long-term temperature normal with a shorter-period warming trend, is left to future revision. Because the topographic screen's own feature set includes mean elevation, the finding that only a narrow upper-elevation fringe of either district's terrain gains thermal suitability under warming (\S\ref{sec:results:overestimation}) should be read alongside this limitation: the screen cannot, by construction, rule out that this fringe gain is partly an artefact of a cell's own elevation moving it away from its district's centroid, rather than purely a genuine agro-ecological signal, and disentangling the two is left to future work. Precipitation is treated implicitly through its correlation with elevation-derived climate features but is not projected as an independent driver. Hawaiian precipitation projections show larger model spread than temperature, and drought stress may co-constrain expansion terrain in either district in ways the current framework does not capture. The topographic model does not incorporate hydrological access, land tenure, or irrigation infrastructure. Some cells scoring well on the topographic screen may be inaccessible for logistical or legal reasons not captured in the eight-feature topographic screen. None of these limitations alter the structural result that terrain constraints bound the feasible adaptation space; they affect the precise location of that space, not its geometric existence.

The same timescale mismatch operates wherever a crop is terrain-anchored, though the arithmetic of the intersection behaves less simply than a naive terrain-fraction argument implies. Using the formal notation from \S\ref{sec:intro}, when $\mathcal{C}_r \supseteq \mathcal{T}_r$---that is, when virtually the entire terrain-feasible footprint is also thermally suitable---$\mathcal{F}_r = \mathcal{T}_r$, and the overestimation factor reduces to its loosest possible value, $|\mathcal{C}_r|/|\mathcal{T}_r|$, bounded above by $|\mathcal{G}|/|\mathcal{T}_r|$, the inverse of the terrain fraction (14.7$\times$ Kona, 11.3$\times$ Ka\okina u). That special case is far from holding here: under the level-set definition (\S\ref{sec:methods}), only 26.1\% of farmable island land exceeds the $\tau=0.5$ threshold under the Kona envelope and 19.8\% under the Ka\okina u envelope (\S\ref{sec:results:overestimation}), so $\mathcal{C}_r$ is a small subset of $\mathcal{G}$, and $|\mathcal{G}|/|\mathcal{T}_r|$ is a loose bound rather than a tight one. What the realized factors (6.9$\times$ Kona, 3.9$\times$ Ka\okina u) instead reveal is that each district's own terrain is \textit{more} thermally suitable than farmable land generally, not less---because coffee was planted on terrain already close to each district's thermal optimum, the terrain-thermal interaction pulls the realized factor \textit{below} the terrain-fraction bound rather than above it. What survives is a narrower point than the specific bound: a district's terrain footprint concentrates on land disproportionately likely to already be climatically favourable, so an island-wide thermal-only estimate overstates the district's own accessible opportunity regardless of the exact multiplier, because it credits land the district's farms were never sited to occupy in the first place; the geometry recurs wherever a specialty crop is anchored to a geographically restricted terrain configuration. In Colombia, OvalleRivera et al.\ \citep{OvalleRivera2015} identify $\approx$21,900\,km$^2$ of currently climatically suitable Arabica area and project the suitable zone shifting 500--1,300\,m upslope under mid-century warming, creating the appearance of large new opportunity. The authors themselves note that conversion of higher-elevation terrain is uncertain, citing soil and access constraints---precisely the terrain feasibility question our framework makes explicit and quantifiable. As in Kona, the upslope terrain above the current Colombian production band (above $\approx$1,500\,m) transitions to younger, steeper, less-developed substrate that may satisfy a thermal criterion while lacking the agro-ecological structure of the existing farms. Ethiopian highland Arabica, grown on Afromontane terrain with specific geological and moisture dynamics, faces an analogous constraint \citep{Davis2012}. The pattern recurs from Kona to Ka\okina u to Colombia: a terrain configuration fixed on geological or geomorphological timescales does not track a thermal optimum moving on a decadal one. The overestimation is not a Hawaiian peculiarity---it is a structural consequence of evaluating climate suitability over a continuous geographic surface when the crop's realized niche is defined by the intersection of climate and a spatially restricted terrain configuration. The magnitude scales with terrain specificity; the correction is the same regardless of crop or region: apply a feasibility screen before interpreting thermal projections as actionable adaptation opportunity.

% ── 5. Conclusions ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Conclusions}

Terrain is fixed on geological timescales; a thermal optimum moves on decadal ones. Where a crop's identity---and its price---is bound to a particular terrain configuration, that mismatch means the optimum can migrate but the crop cannot follow it.

Hawaiian coffee makes the mismatch legible because both sides of it can be measured. Only 5.9\% of the Big Island carries Kona's agro-ecological terrain signature and 8.9\% carries Ka\okina u's, and those footprints are properties of the volcano rather than of the climate. Against them, the thermally suitable area island-wide is essentially static between 2035 and 2045---down 0.6\% under one district's envelope, up 0.5\% under the other's---while the intersection that actually matters contracts by 15.5\% and 12.4\%. The loss is produced entirely by the two sets sliding out of register, and it falls hardest on land already in production: 72\% of Kona's and 77\% of Ka\okina u's farm cells are on a declining thermal trajectory by 2035, and 79\% and 86\% by 2045. A climate-only reading of the same warming would report roughly 70\% of farmable island land improving, a figure that is very nearly a restatement of how much of the island lies above 600\,m.

The convergence map (Figure~\ref{fig:convergence}) is the decision surface for adaptation planning in both districts---not where coffee might theoretically exist under future temperatures, but where it actually can. Kona and Ka\okina u are both spatially constrained, in the same way and to a similar degree; this is one problem with two local expressions, not two different problems, and relying on thermal projections alone would misdirect both the research investment and the adaptation response for either district.

The argument is transferable wherever a crop's market identity is anchored to a terrain configuration rather than merely to a climate: designation-of-origin viticulture, highland Arabica systems, and any product whose premium survives only on particular ground. In each case the correct question under warming is not how much land becomes climatically suitable, but how long the suitable band and the identity-bearing terrain remain in register---and, when they separate, whether the crop's value can survive the move. That is a question about the intersection of a moving set and a fixed one, and it cannot be answered from a thermal projection alone.

