% ── 6. Methods ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Methods}
\label{sec:methods}

\subsection{Data}
\label{sec:data}

\subsubsection{Geospatial Grid and Farm Boundaries}

Farm boundaries were sourced from the 2020 Hawai\okina i Statewide Agricultural Land Use Baseline \citep{PerroyCollier2021} (674 coffee polygons, $\approx$6{,}000\,acres total), projected to UTM Zone~4N and intersected with a 500\,m grid to yield 471 grid cells labelled by farm-centroid overlap. All 471 cells (Kona $n = 409$, Ka\okina u $n = 62$) are used throughout the analysis below. The two quantities estimated from the farm sample---the topographic centroids (\S\ref{sec:methods:topo}) and the thermal envelope parameters (\S\ref{sec:methods:thermal})---require only each cell's terrain features, sampled from the island DEM, and its temperature, sampled from the gridded climatology; both are available for all 471 cells.

Two exclusions applied in an earlier version of this analysis have been removed, and we record the second because it is not a neutral omission. A requirement for complete SSURGO texture variables removed 68 cells; those variables enter no feature set used here. A requirement for complete ERA5-Land extraction removed a further 86. ERA5-Land is a $\approx$9\,km land product whose land--sea mask does not resolve the narrow Kona coastal strip, and the affected cells returned no ERA5-Land data at all---every variable, in all 108 monthly composites. They are not a random subset: they are the low, warm, seaward margin of the Kona belt (mean elevation 327\,m and mean temperature 21.9\,\textdegree C as measured by the Kodama climatology, which does resolve them, against 535\,m and 20.8\,\textdegree C for the retained cells; $p < 10^{-5}$ for both), which is the population most relevant to a claim about where farms sit relative to their thermal optimum. Since the 250\,m climatology resolves that strip and ERA5-Land now contributes only an island-wide $\Delta T$, per-cell ERA5 completeness is no longer required. Island-wide projections were computed across all valid 500\,m land cells above sea level.

\subsubsection{Terrain Features}

Elevation data from the USGS 3D Elevation Program (3DEP) 1/3 arc-second DEM \citep{USGS3DEP} were mosaic-merged across three tiles and average-resampled to 500\,m. Eight topographic features were computed per cell: mean elevation, elevation standard deviation, maximum slope, aspect sine, aspect cosine, total relief, local relief, and distance to coastline.

\subsubsection{Climate}

Baseline climate was sourced from ERA5-Land daily reanalysis \citep{HersbachERA5}, aggregated to 18 annual and seasonal features for 2017--2025. Future projections used delta-method bias correction applied to ACCESS-CM2 and MPI-ESM1-2-HR under SSP2-4.5 and SSP5-8.5, obtained from the NASA NEX-GDDP-CMIP6 archive \citep{Thrasher2022}, for 20-year windows centred on 2035 and 2045. Model-ensemble means within each scenario are $\Delta T_{2035} = +0.60$\,\textdegree C and $\Delta T_{2045} = +0.92$\,\textdegree C under SSP2-4.5, and $+1.41$ and $+1.78$\,\textdegree C under SSP5-8.5.

Because SSP2-4.5 and SSP5-8.5 are alternative forcing pathways rather than ensemble members, we do not average across them: their mean corresponds to no emissions trajectory and should not be presented as a projection. All headline quantities are instead reported under each scenario separately (Supplementary Table~S2), retaining model-ensemble averaging (ACCESS-CM2 $\times$ MPI-ESM1-2-HR) within each. Maps and the figures derived from them are shown at stated reference increments of $+1.00$ and $+1.35$\,\textdegree C, which bracket the 2035 and 2045 scenario range and are labelled as increments rather than as scenario outcomes; readers wishing a specific pathway should use Supplementary Table~S2.

$\Delta T$ is applied uniformly in space. This is a constraint of the source data rather than a modelling choice: NEX-GDDP-CMIP6 is distributed on a $0.25$\textdegree\ ($\approx$25\,km) grid, on which the entire Island of Hawai\okina i falls within essentially one cell, and the extracted $\Delta T$ is identical to four decimal places across all farm cells. No spatially resolved warming signal is available at the scale of a coffee district from this product.

Uniform $\Delta T$ nonetheless warrants scrutiny, because elevation-dependent warming is documented in Hawai\okina i. The strongest such signal is associated with the trade-wind inversion, whose base sits near $2{,}150$--$2{,}225$\,m \citep{KagawaVivianiGiambelluca2020}; both coffee districts lie entirely below it, spanning roughly 200--950\,m, so the free-atmosphere and high-elevation trends that dominate the Hawaiian elevation-dependent-warming literature do not apply directly to the belt. To bound the residual risk we repeated the analysis with an imposed elevation gradient, $\Delta T(z) = \Delta T_0 + g\,(z - \bar{z})$, holding the belt-mean increment fixed and varying $g$ from 0 to $2$\,\textdegree C\,km$^{-1}$. Because the observed temperature field falls at $\approx-5.05$\,\textdegree C\,km$^{-1}$ (\S\ref{sec:methods:thermal}), positive $g$ flattens the effective lapse to $(5.05 - g)$, raising the gain isotherm and \textit{shrinking} the upper-elevation gaining fringe. We test this against the quantities the paper actually rests on---$|\mathcal{F}_r|$ and its horizon-to-horizon contraction---rather than against the island-wide gain fraction, which is hypsometric (\S\ref{sec:results:thermal}). Increasing $g$ enlarges the feasible set slightly at each horizon but steepens the contraction between them: for Kona the 2035--2045 change moves from $-15.5\%$ at $g = 0$ to $-17.3\%$, $-18.7\%$ and $-22.3\%$ at $g = 0.5$, $1.0$ and $2.0$\,\textdegree C\,km$^{-1}$; for Ka\okina u from $-12.4\%$ to $-15.8\%$, $-18.5\%$ and $-22.8\%$. Spatially uniform $\Delta T$ is therefore the conservative assumption with respect to this paper's central claim: any realistic elevation dependence would make the feasible set contract faster, not slower.

\subsubsection{Variables Extracted but Not Used}

Median monthly Sentinel-2 NDVI composites (2017--2025, via Google Earth Engine \citep{Gorelick2017}) and eight SSURGO soil properties at 0--30\,cm \citep{SSURGO} were extracted for the farm cells during an earlier, classification-oriented version of this work. Neither enters the topographic screen, the thermal envelope, or any quantity reported here, and we do not report them as inputs. They are retained in the public data release for reproducibility only. A feasibility screen that incorporated soil and water availability directly---rather than relying on terrain as a proxy for the agro-ecological configuration those variables partly constitute---would be a stronger instrument than the one used here, and is the most obvious extension of this work (\S\ref{sec:discussion:limits}).

\subsubsection{Surficial Geology}
\label{sec:data:geology}

Lava-flow age was sourced from the digital geologic database of the Island of Hawai\okina i \citep{Trusdell2006}, which digitises the underlying 1:100{,}000 geologic map of Wolfe and Morris (USGS Geologic Investigations Series I-2524-A, 1996); we used the National Park Service Geologic Resources Inventory shapefile reformatting of that database, comprising 6{,}141 mapped rock-unit polygons joined to their legend entries by unit symbol. Polygons mapped as ``Historic A.D.\ 1790 or younger''---the youngest, least-weathered substrate, including the 1859 and 1950 Mauna Loa flows and the bulk of Kīlauea's recent output---were rasterised onto the same 500\,m UTM grid as the island DEM (\S\ref{sec:methods:farmability}).

\subsection{Topographic Feasibility Screen}
\label{sec:methods:topo}

Eight topographic features (\S\ref{sec:data}) were standardised using a \texttt{StandardScaler} fitted on the 471 labelled farm cells, and regional centroids were computed as the mean standardised feature vector for each district. The feature set---elevation mean, elevation standard deviation, maximum slope, aspect sine, aspect cosine, total relief, local relief, and coastline distance---captures the primary axes of topographic variation that distinguish cultivated terrain on the Big Island: elevation band, slope, aspect orientation, landscape roughness, and coastal proximity. For each island cell, Euclidean distance to each centroid was converted to a continuous similarity score:
\[
    s_r = \frac{1}{1 + d_r}, \quad r \in \{\text{Kona},\; \text{Ka\textquoteleft u}\}
\]
A topographic identity score $\text{identity} = s_{\text{Ka'u}} - s_{\text{Kona}}$ assigns each cell to the closer regional profile: positive values indicate Ka\okina u-like terrain, negative values indicate Kona-like terrain. Because $s_r = 1/(1+d_r)$ is monotone in $d_r$, this score changes no assignment that the raw distances would not also make; only its sign is interpreted. Its magnitude is a compressed, non-linear function of a distance difference and carries no units, so the numerical values on the Figure~\ref{fig:identity} colour scale should be read as an ordering rather than as a measured quantity.

Euclidean distance in standardised feature space is appropriate here because the objective is domain bounding rather than probabilistic classification: the screen asks whether a cell falls within the cloud of observed farm terrain, not which class it is most likely to belong to. Standardisation removes scale differences across features; the remaining correlation structure is treated as representing genuinely co-occurring terrain constraints rather than redundancy to be decorrelated. As a robustness check, we repeated the analysis using Mahalanobis distance with a pooled within-class covariance matrix, which fully decorrelates the feature space before computing distances. The Mahalanobis screen produces a smaller Ka\okina u terrain footprint (3.76\% vs.\ 8.90\% at the 95th-percentile baseline), consistent with several features being correlated on island terrain (elevation and coastline distance co-vary; total and local relief overlap). At the 95th-percentile baseline the Mahalanobis screen gives the larger overestimation factor (12.2$\times$ vs.\ 3.9$\times$ Euclidean, level-set definition, \S\ref{sec:methods}), because its smaller footprint excludes a larger share of the thermally-suitable region; the two metrics converge toward each other as the threshold widens (27.7$\times$ Mahalanobis vs.\ 6.7$\times$ Euclidean at the 90th percentile, narrowing to 8.9$\times$ vs.\ 3.2$\times$ at the 97.5th; Supplementary Table~S1). Both metrics agree that terrain-constrained suitability is several-fold smaller than thermal-only suitability at every threshold tested, but the specific factor is sensitive to distance-metric choice and should not be read as a precise, metric-independent number.

This defines a feasibility constraint: terrain unlike existing farms is excluded from suitability scoring regardless of thermal conditions. The screen captures realized agro-ecological structure, not the full fundamental niche, and is intended as a constraint on economically and operationally viable expansion, not biological possibility. The framework is not a predictive classifier of suitability, but a constraint on the geographic domain over which suitability can be meaningfully evaluated. Stability of the classification under threshold perturbation (Supplementary Fig.~S1) is a robustness check, not a validation: it establishes that the screen is insensitive to the cutoff, not that it identifies terrain capable of supporting coffee. An out-of-sample test is reported in \S\ref{sec:methods:kauai}.

Cells where distance to the nearer centroid exceeds the 95th percentile of within-region farm-to-centroid distances are excluded; this threshold is interpreted as a boundary of observed agro-ecological coherence rather than a statistical cutoff per se. A sensitivity analysis (90th--97.5th percentile range) shows Jaccard similarity to the 95th-pct baseline of 0.82 (Kona) / 0.79 (Ka\okina u) at the 92.5th percentile and 0.86 / 0.81 at the 97.5th, falling to 0.66 / 0.57 at the 90th (Supplementary Fig.~S1). Ka\okina u is the less stable of the two at every threshold, consistent with its centroid being estimated from only 62 farm cells; we read the drop at the 90th percentile as tail instability in a distance distribution estimated from few points, not as an ecological boundary. Critically, the overestimation factor itself---the ratio of thermal-only to terrain-constrained suitable area under the level-set definition (\S\ref{sec:methods})---ranges from 3.2$\times$ (97.5th percentile) to 6.7$\times$ (90th percentile) under Euclidean distance across this sensitivity window (Supplementary Table~S1). That specific magnitude is not threshold-invariant---it is set jointly by the terrain footprint size and by how much of the thermally-suitable area falls inside it---but the terrain footprint is small at every threshold tested (5.0--11.0\% of island land classifying as Ka\okina u-like across the 90th--97.5th percentile range), and a several-fold overestimation follows as a structural consequence, not an artefact of the specific threshold chosen. The screen classifies 5.9\% of island land as Kona-like and 8.9\% as Ka\okina u-like at the 95th-percentile baseline; the remaining 85.1\% shares neither district's agro-ecological signature.

\subsection{Out-of-Sample Test on Kaua\okina i}
\label{sec:methods:kauai}

The screen is fitted entirely on Big Island farm cells, so Kaua\okina i provides an independent test. The same 2020 Statewide Agricultural Land Use Baseline \citep{PerroyCollier2021} records ten commercial coffee polygons on Kaua\okina i totalling $\approx$3{,}806 acres---a single estate larger than Ka\okina u's entire district footprint---on an island the fitting procedure never sees. We mosaicked the same USGS 3DEP 1/3 arc-second product for Kaua\okina i, reprojected and resampled it to the same 500\,m UTM grid, computed the same eight topographic features by the same code path, and applied the Big Island centroids and 95th-percentile distance threshold unchanged.

The screen classifies 224 of 5{,}986 Kaua\okina i land cells (3.7\%) as Kona-like or Ka\okina u-like, and \textit{none} of them coincides with the 61 cells whose centres fall inside a mapped coffee polygon. Recall is zero, precision is zero, and the enrichment over Kaua\okina i's 1.0\% base rate of coffee land is zero. Repeating the test using only the six features that are not defined relative to the containing island---dropping elevation deviation from scene mean and distance to coastline, both of which are bounded by island size---does not change the outcome.

The reason is substantive rather than technical. Kaua\okina i's coffee sits at a mean elevation of 84\,m on 7\textdegree\ slopes; Kona and Ka\okina u farm cells average 494\,m and 524\,m on 19\textdegree\ and 17\textdegree\ slopes. It is a low-elevation coastal plantation, not a mid-elevation volcanic slope system, and the screen rejects it because it genuinely does not resemble the terrain the screen was built from.

We report this as a negative result and draw the narrow conclusion it supports. The screen does not identify coffee-growing terrain in general; it identifies Kona- and Ka\okina u-like terrain specifically, and the largest coffee estate in the United States falls entirely outside it. That is consistent with the framework's stated purpose---an identity-anchored screen for a designation-of-origin crop, not an arability mask (\S\ref{sec:intro})---and it is evidence that ``coffee country'' is not a single terrain signature: Kaua\okina i coffee and Kona coffee occupy different agro-ecological settings, and command different prices. But it is not positive confirmation that the screen identifies \textit{viable} terrain, and no such confirmation is available from this test. Claims in \S\ref{sec:results:convergence} about upper-fringe cells being candidates for feasibility study should be read with that absence in mind.

\subsection{Thermal Envelope Model}
\label{sec:methods:thermal}

Island-wide temperature is estimated from the Kodama et al.\ \citep{Kodama2024} Hawai\okina i climate atlas, a 250\,m gridded monthly temperature product (1990--2021, statewide) built from the Hawai\okina i Climate Data Portal's spatial modelling framework, rather than from a fixed lapse rate or a regression calibrated on the farm cells themselves. We obtained monthly mean-temperature rasters for the Big Island for the product's full 1990--2021 record (384 months) and averaged them into a single climatological-mean raster. A regression fit directly to the farm cells is not used because Kona and Ka\okina u occupy different microclimates on opposite flanks of the volcano, and elevation alone does not predict farm-cell temperature within either district in isolation ($R^2 \approx 0.01$ for a within-district ordinary-least-squares fit against ERA5-Land); a regression pooled across both districts inherits that noise and, when extrapolated island-wide, produces a physically implausible gradient. ERA5-Land's 9\,km reanalysis grid is likewise too coarse to resolve a within-district gradient, so it is not used for the temperature surface either. The Kodama climatology was reprojected from its native geographic (EPSG:4326) grid to the same 500\,m UTM Zone~4N grid as the island DEM (\S\ref{sec:data}) via bilinear resampling, so that the island-wide surface used for $\Delta S_r$ (below) and the temperature sampled at each farm cell's own location are drawn from one consistent grid and scale, rather than mixing a coarse and a fine product. Farm-cell temperatures were sampled from this same 500\,m grid at each cell's UTM centroid. Over Big Island land the climatology ranges 5.6--23.8\,\textdegree C, consistent with the $\approx$22--24\,\textdegree C statewide sea-level mean and the $\approx-6$ to $-6.5$\,\textdegree C\,km$^{-1}$ lapse behaviour independently reported for the islands below the $\approx$2{,}150\,m trade-wind inversion \citep{KagawaVivianiGiambelluca2020, GiambellucaNullet1991}, cross-checking the gridded product against the literature values the earlier lapse-rate model was anchored to. Aspect-driven and inversion-layer microclimates not fully captured at 500\,m are discussed in \S\ref{sec:discussion:limits}.

Separate Gaussian thermal envelopes approximate each district's thermal niche, consistent with unimodal species--environment response assumptions widely used in ecological modelling \citep{Guisan2000}:
\[
    S_r(T,\,\Delta T) = \exp\!\left[-\frac{1}{2}
    \left(\frac{T + \Delta T - \mu_r}{\sigma_r}\right)^2\right]
\]
where $\mu_r$ and $\sigma_r$ are the mean and $1.5\times$ the standard deviation of the Kodama climatology temperature sampled at each farm cell's own location (above) across the 471 farm cells in region $r$ ($\mu_{\text{Kona}}=21.00$\,\textdegree C, $\sigma_{\text{Kona}}=1.37$\,\textdegree C, $n=409$; $\mu_{\text{Ka'u}}=20.82$\,\textdegree C, $\sigma_{\text{Ka'u}}=1.04$\,\textdegree C, $n=62$). Because $\mu_r$ and $\sigma_r$ are computed on the identical surface used to evaluate $S_r$ everywhere else, both are expressed on one consistent temperature scale throughout. The $1.5\times$ inflation reflects within-cell and farm-scale microclimatic variability---shade cover, canopy structure, and very local aspect effects---that is not resolved at 500\,m grid resolution even by a real observed climatology. The inflation factor is not intended to expand the fundamental niche but to approximate unresolved sub-grid variability in microclimate conditions at farm scale; it is a judgement, not a calibrated quantity, and we therefore report its consequences directly. Because $\sigma_r$ does not affect the sign of $\Delta S_r$ under a Gaussian envelope (below), the inflation changes no reported gain/loss cell count or percentage. It does move the level set $\mathcal{C}_r$ and hence the overestimation factor, though modestly: varying the inflation across $1.0\times$, $1.25\times$, $1.5\times$ and $2.0\times$ moves the 2035 factor from 6.9$\times$ to 7.1$\times$ (Kona) and 3.6$\times$ to 4.2$\times$ (Ka\okina u), a smaller spread than either the terrain-threshold or distance-metric choice produces (\S\ref{sec:results:overestimation}). This is a deliberate simplification: using the same thermal model, on the same temperature scale, for both districts and for both the terrain-constrained and unconstrained analyses ensures that the overestimation factor reflects the terrain screen alone, not any residual from model complexity or from mismatched temperature references.

Two properties of this construction constrain how its outputs should be read, and we state both explicitly rather than leave them to be discovered. First, for a Gaussian envelope $\sigma_r$ cancels out of the \textit{sign} of the gain/loss classification regardless of how $T$ is estimated: $\Delta S_r(h) > 0 \iff T < \mu_r - \Delta T_h/2$, independent of $\sigma_r$, for any $T$. $\sigma_r$ therefore affects the \textit{magnitude} of $\Delta S_r$---and hence the level set $\mathcal{C}_r$ and the colour scales in Figures~\ref{fig:thermal}--\ref{fig:convergence}---but never which cells gain or lose. Second, and more seriously, the thermal and terrain screens are not independent, and the degree of dependence is greater at the scale this analysis operates than an island-wide statistic suggests. Regressing the Kodama climatology on elevation across all Big Island land gives $R^2 = 0.98$ (implied lapse $-5.05$\,\textdegree C\,km$^{-1}$, residual SD $0.60$\,\textdegree C). That residual, however, is dominated by curvature in the temperature--elevation relation across the full $0$--$4{,}166$\,m span, most of which lies far above the coffee belt. Restricted to the elevation band the districts actually occupy ($150$--$950$\,m) the fit is $R^2 = 0.999$ with a residual standard deviation of $0.032$\,\textdegree C, and within each district's own farm cells it is $R^2 = 0.9995$ (Kona) and $0.998$ (Ka\okina u). Regressing the residual on aspect explains under 1\% of it. For comparison, that residual is 43 times smaller than $\sigma_{\text{Kona}}$ and 31 times smaller than $\Delta T_{2035}$: it is far below the scale of any quantity this analysis resolves.

This is a property of the product, not an accident of our sampling. The Kodama climatology is constructed by optimised piecewise-linear regression on elevation \citep{Kodama2024}, so within a single narrow elevation band it cannot carry information independent of elevation by construction. We therefore withdraw any claim that adopting an observed gridded climatology recovers aspect or cloud-belt structure at the district scale; it does not, and no gridded product built this way could. Replacing the lapse rate removed an \textit{assumed} elevation dependence in favour of an \textit{observed} one, which is worth doing---the lapse rate, intercept and any curvature are now measured rather than imposed---but it does not make the thermal screen independent of the terrain screen's elevation feature. Within the belt, temperature is elevation. The consequence is that the boundary remains close to a single isotherm and hence close to a single elevation contour (\S\ref{sec:results:thermal}), and the near-identical island-wide gain fractions for the two districts follow directly from their near-identical $\mu_r$. Claims of independence between the two screens would not be supportable, and none are made.

Thermal suitability change at horizon $h$ is:
\[
    \Delta S_r(h) = S_r(T,\,\Delta T_h) - S_r(T,\,0)
\]
Positive $\Delta S$ indicates warming toward greater alignment with the thermal optimum; negative values indicate reduced alignment.

A consequence of this construction deserves explicit statement, since it bounds what the spatial analysis can claim. Because $\mu_r$ is the mean of the same farm-cell temperature distribution against which $\Delta S_r$ is then evaluated, and because the gain condition is $T < \mu_r - \Delta T_h/2$, the declining fraction of a district's farm cells is approximately $\Phi\!\left(\Delta T_h / 2 s_r\right)$, where $s_r$ is the uninflated standard deviation of that distribution and $\Phi$ the standard normal CDF. The approximation is close: for Kona at 2035 the closed form gives 70.8\% against an observed 72.1\%, and for Ka\okina u 76.5\% against 77.4\% (2045: 77.0\% vs 78.5\% and 83.5\% vs 85.5\%). The declining-fraction statistic is therefore substantially a restatement of the ratio between the warming increment and the within-district temperature spread, and it would take a similar value for any crop, region, or warming signal with a comparable ratio. What the gridded analysis contributes is not this scalar but its spatial expression: \textit{which} cells decline, whether they coincide with land that is actually farmed, and---the subject of this paper---whether the cells that improve fall inside or outside the district's terrain footprint. We report the fraction because it is interpretable, not because it is a spatial finding.

Two distinct quantities use this suitability score in what follows, and we keep them explicitly separate. The \textit{direction-of-change} statistic, $\Delta S_r(h)$ above, classifies each cell as gaining or losing suitability under warming, independent of its absolute suitability level; this is what \S\ref{sec:results:overestimation}'s district-specific gain/loss percentages and \S\ref{sec:results:thermal}'s island-wide gain fractions report. The \textit{thermal suitability set} $\mathcal{C}_r(\Delta T)$ introduced formally in \S\ref{sec:intro}, by contrast, is a level set: $\mathcal{C}_r(\Delta T) = \{g \in \mathcal{G} : S_r(T_g, \Delta T) > \tau\}$, the farmable cells whose absolute suitability under future warming exceeds a threshold $\tau$, regardless of whether that represents a gain or a loss relative to baseline. $\mathcal{C}_r(\Delta T)$, not $\Delta S_r(h) > 0$, is what defines the overestimation factor in \S\ref{sec:results:overestimation}: a cell can register $\Delta S_r > 0$ while remaining thermally unsuitable in absolute terms (a cold, high-elevation cell warming from a suitability score near 0 to a still-negligible score is technically ``gaining''), and conflating that with genuine thermal suitability would inflate the apparent thermal-only opportunity with land that was never realistically viable. We use $\tau = 0.5$ as the primary threshold and report sensitivity over $\tau = 0.1$--$0.8$ in \S\ref{sec:results:overestimation}.

\subsection{Farmability Screen}
\label{sec:methods:farmability}

All projections are restricted to farmable terrain: maximum slope $\leq 25$\textdegree, excluding cliff margins, \textit{and} not mapped as a lava flow of Historic (A.D.\ 1790 or younger) age (\S\ref{sec:data:geology})---slope alone does not exclude young, barren, unconsolidated lava fields, which can be flat enough to pass a slope-only screen while remaining agriculturally unusable. Of island land, 84.8\% satisfies both criteria: 94.3\% passes the slope criterion alone, and the historic-lava exclusion removes a further 9.5 percentage points (4{,}044 cells; a further 149 historic-lava cells sit on terrain already excluded as too steep, so the two criteria overlap slightly and 9.9\% of island land is historic lava in total). The excluded flows are concentrated on Mauna Loa's southwest rift and Kīlauea's east and southwest rift zones.

\subsection{Uncertainty Quantification}
\label{sec:methods:uncertainty}

Confidence intervals on farm-derived quantities are obtained by bootstrap. Because farm cells are spatially contiguous---each district is a single connected belt---an independent resample of cells would treat spatially redundant observations as independent and understate the interval. We therefore use a spatial block bootstrap: within each district, farm cells are partitioned into compact spatial blocks by $k$-means on projected coordinates, targeting approximately eight cells per block (Kona: 409 cells in 51 blocks; Ka\okina u: 62 cells in 8 blocks), and blocks are resampled with replacement. The distinction is material: block intervals are 1.8--2.4 times wider than independent-resample intervals for the same statistic, and the independent version should be regarded as anticonservative.

For the overestimation factor the entire screen is refitted within each replicate---feature standardisation, both district centroids, the 95th-percentile distance threshold, and the thermal envelope parameters $\mu_r$ and $\sigma_r$---so that the interval propagates uncertainty in the terrain screen itself rather than holding it fixed. Reported intervals are 2.5th--97.5th percentiles over 2{,}000 replicates for both the factor and the decline fractions. An earlier run at 400 replicates gave a materially narrower upper bound for the factor (Kona 8.8$\times$ rather than 9.1$\times$; Ka\okina u 7.9$\times$ rather than 8.6$\times$), which is expected for a right-skewed ratio whose upper percentile is estimated from few draws.

Two features of the resulting intervals are worth noting. Ka\okina u's are substantially wider than Kona's throughout, which follows directly from its smaller and more spatially concentrated sample: 62 contiguous cells support roughly eight independent blocks, and no analysis can extract more resolution than that supports. And the factor intervals are asymmetric, with a long upper tail (Ka\okina u: 3.2--8.6 around a point estimate of 3.9), because the factor is a ratio whose denominator---the terrain-constrained suitable area---can become small in replicates that happen to sample a narrow terrain envelope. This asymmetry is a further reason not to treat the factor as a stable scalar (\S\ref{sec:results:overestimation}).

\subsection{Terrain-Thermal Convergence Analysis}

The terrain-thermal convergence map is the study's primary analytical product (Figure~\ref{fig:convergence}). For each farmable cell, thermal suitability change is displayed only if the cell passes the topographic feasibility screen, and is colour-coded by the matched regional envelope: Kona-like cells receive $\Delta S_{\text{Kona}}$; Ka\okina u-like cells receive $\Delta S_{\text{Ka'u}}$. Farmable cells outside both profiles are shown in mid-grey---these cells may experience thermally favourable futures but lack the terrain, soil, and microclimate structure of existing coffee country. Steep terrain appears in light grey. The coloured footprint is the only geographic space where thermal adaptation signals are simultaneously grounded in terrain reality.

